# Plan: eligibility income, outgoings and property routing

| | |
|---|---|
| Status | Draft |
| Author | Will Clarke |
| Reviewers | _To be added_ |
| Last updated | 2026-10-19 |

## Summary

The non-financial section routes correctly. The income, outgoings and property sections do not.

The step order logic is not the problem. `src/eligibility/domain/` is a faithful port of CCQ's `Steps::Logic`, `Steps::Helper` and the six `Steps::*Section` classes (pinned at CCQ commit `1a30ea3f`, which is still CCQ `main`). The problem is that the **Forge step definitions under `src/eligibility/journey/` save different answer keys and option values from the ones the domain logic reads**. The branch predicates then never fire, so steps are skipped or never reached.

The non-financial blocks were built against CCQ's form classes (same field codes, same option values), so they work. The other three sections were built as placeholders (one text box per step) and were not.

This plan maps how each section works in CCQ, what the operator app has today, and the gaps. Each section is a separate piece of work.

## Decisions

| # | Decision | Date |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Amount and frequency fields are **always shown**, not revealed by the preceding yes/no. They are validated only when the yes/no is "yes". Revisit conditional reveal later (Forge supports it with `visibleWhen`) | 2026-10-19 |
| 2 | The state benefit type list for `benefit_details` and `partner_benefit_details` comes **from CFE** at request time. No static list to maintain | 2026-10-19 |
| 3 | `rented` is dropped. The main-home question has four options, and the fourth is "No" with the CCQ value `none` | 2026-10-19 |
| 4 | If CFE does not return a benefit type list (the call fails, or the list is empty), the operator sees an **error page** so they can report the problem. Do not fall back to an empty list as CCQ does | 2026-10-19 |
| 5 | Existing sessions do not matter. The service is not live, so any saved sessions are test data and field codes can change freely | 2026-10-19 |

## How routing works

### In CCQ

- `Steps::Helper` (`app/lib/steps/helper.rb`) rebuilds the ordered step list from the whole session on every request. Sections run in a fixed order: NonFinancial, Income, Partner, Outgoings, Property, AssetsAndVehicles.
- Each section returns **groups** of step codes. A group is a run of steps handled together in the change-answers loop.
- Every conditional comes from predicates in `Steps::Logic` that read **session keys written by the forms**, for example `employment_status == "in_work"`, `property_owned in [outright, with_mortgage, shared_ownership]`, `additional_property_owned`.
- The next step is the one after the current step in that list. `cannot_use_service?` overrides it for unsupported shared-ownership answers.

### In this app

- `domain/navigation.ts` and `domain/sections/*.ts` port the above one-to-one.
- `registries/effects.ts` `publishNavigation` runs after every load and save. It builds a `nav` map of step to next path from `stepsFor(answers)`. Each step redirects with `Data("nav.<step>")`.
- Answers are saved under the Forge **field code**. The domain reads that same key, so **the field code and option values must be exactly CCQ's**.
- `domain/step-answer-keys.ts` already lists the CCQ keys for every step. It is the contract for field codes. `domain/cfe/*` also reads CCQ-shaped answers (`incomes`, `benefits`, `housing_loan_payments`, `partner_incomes`, ...).

### What goes wrong (verified with `stepsFor`)

| Block saves | Domain reads | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `employment_status` = `not_in_work` / `self_employed` | `in_work` / `unemployed` | `self_employed` never reaches `income` |
| `additional_property` = `true` | `additional_property_owned` = `none` / `outright` / `with_mortgage` / `shared_ownership` | `additional_property_details` never reached. Shared-ownership "cannot use service" never fires |
| `property_owned` = `rented` | `none` | Works by accident for routing, but the value is not CCQ's. Replace with "No" / `none` (decision 3) |
| `partner_benefits` (yes/no) | `partner_receives_benefits` | Partner benefit-details routing never fires |
| No `partner_additional_property*` steps exist | Domain emits them when there is a partner | `nav` points at a path with no step |
| `housing_costs`, `mortgage_or_loan_payment`, `shared_ownership_housing_costs`, `partner_outgoings` (one text box each) | 2 to 12 fields each (see below) | Answers are dropped by `relevantAnswers`, so CFE gets nothing |
| `income`, `benefit_details`, `other_income` single fields | `incomes[]`, `benefits[]`, 16 `*_relevant` / `*_conditional_value` / `*_frequency` fields | Same: dropped, and `childcare` eligibility cannot be computed |

## Approach for every section

1. Make the Forge fields use the CCQ field codes, option values and validation from the form class. The CCQ form is the spec. The CCQ slim view is the spec for conditional reveals.
2. Do not change `domain/sections/*` or `domain/logic.ts` unless a parity test proves a difference. They are correct.
3. Register any new step in `journey/index.ts` and give it a `Data("nav.<code>")` redirect. The step code must be the CCQ code, and the path must be the code with `_` as `-`.
4. Convert yes/no with the existing `toDomainAnswers` (`"true"`/`"false"` to booleans). Do not hand-roll.
5. Show amount and frequency fields all the time (decision 1). Keep the existing "validate only when the yes/no is yes" conditions (`poundsAmountWhenAnswered` and friends). Add `dependentWhen` on those fields so a value left behind after switching back to "no" is cleared on save. `dependentWhen` does not change rendering.
6. For add-another lists, follow the bank-accounts pattern (`Data("bankAccountRows").each`, `AddBankAccountRow` effect, `bankAccountsFromFormAnswers`). Generalise it rather than copying it a third time. See "Shared work".
7. Add or extend: a `stepsFor` routing test, a block field-code test, a short Playwright spec (see [Test strategy](#test-strategy)), and the check-your-answers summary entry.

## Section 1: Income

Do this first. Outgoings needs its output.

### CCQ behaviour

`Steps::IncomeSection`: skipped entirely when `skip_income_questions?` (non-means-tested or passported).

```
employment_status
[income]                  only if employment_status == "in_work"
benefits
[benefit_details]         only if receives_benefits
other_income
```

Groups: `[employment_status]`, `[income]`, `[benefits, benefit_details]`, `[other_income]`.

| Step | CCQ form | Session keys | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `employment_status` | `EmploymentStatusForm` | `employment_status` | Options `in_work`, `unemployed`. Required |
| `income` | `IncomeForm` + `IncomeModel` (add-another) | `incomes[]` of `income_type`, `income_frequency`, `gross_income`, `income_tax`, `national_insurance` | Type: `employment`, `statutory_pay`, `self_employment`. Frequency: `every_week`, `every_two_weeks`, `every_four_weeks`, `monthly`, `three_months`, plus `year` only when certificated. `gross_income > 0`, tax and NI `>= 0`, net must be `> 0`. Type hints depend on level of help and on partner vs client |
| `benefits` | `BenefitsForm` | `receives_benefits` | Boolean |
| `benefit_details` | `BenefitDetailsForm` + `BenefitModel` (add-another) | `benefits[]` of `benefit_type`, `benefit_amount`, `benefit_frequency` | Types come from CFE `GET /state_benefit_type`, minus an omit list and entries with `exclude_from_gross_income`, de-duplicated by `name` (CCQ `BenefitDetailsForm#benefit_list`). CCQ returns an empty list if the call fails. Frequency: `every_week`, `every_two_weeks`, `every_four_weeks`, `monthly`. Amount `> 0` |
| `other_income` | `OtherIncomeForm` | `{friends_or_family,maintenance,property_or_lodger,pension}_{relevant,conditional_value,frequency}`, `student_finance_{relevant,conditional_value}`, `other_{relevant,conditional_value}` | Each `*_relevant` is boolean. Value `> 0` and frequency required when relevant. Frequencies: `every_week`, `every_two_weeks`, `every_four_weeks`, `monthly`, `total` (`total` removed for controlled work). Student finance and other have no frequency. Early gross-income result follows this step (already tracked as Phase 4 in the main plan) |

### Today

`journey/employment-status`, `income`, `benefits`, `benefit-details`, `other-income`. Wrong options on `employment_status`. Single free-text field for `income`, `benefit_details` and a single yes/no for `other_income`. `benefits` (`receives_benefits`) is already correct.

### Tasks

1. `employment_status`: options `in_work` / `unemployed` with CCQ labels. Smallest change, and fixes the routing bug.
2. `income`: add-another rows with the five fields, level-of-help-dependent frequency list and type hints, net-positive validation.
3. `benefit_details` and `partner_benefit_details`: add-another rows with a benefit type select fed from CFE (decision 2).
   - `Assessor.stateBenefitTypes()` and `CfeHttpClient.stateBenefitTypes()` already exist but nothing calls them yet. The existing `StateBenefitType` type (`code`, `label`, `category_description`) does not match the fields CCQ reads (`name`, `label`, `exclude_from_gross_income`). Check the live response and fix the type first.
   - Fetch in `LoadAnswers` (only when the step being loaded is a benefit-details step) and publish with `context.setData("benefitTypes", ...)`. The select reads it with `Data("benefitTypes")`.
   - Port the omit list and filter from CCQ into `domain/` with a unit test.
   - Failure behaviour (decision 4): if the fetch throws or returns an empty list, show an error page instead of the form. The page should say the benefit list could not be loaded from CFE, tell the operator to report it, and let them go back. Include enough detail to raise the issue (the step, CFE status or message, and the case ID). Log the same detail with `console.error`. Do not swallow the error, and do not use the existing `views/main/error.njk` as is: it is login-specific ("Back to login").
   - Implementation: add a small step outside the domain step list, like `cannot_use_service` (`benefit_types_unavailable`, with its own block and no nav entry). The fetch happens in the benefit-details step's access hook. On failure, redirect to that step. Spike how to redirect from an access hook or effect in Forge (`throwError` and `redirect` outcomes), and add the case to `tests/forge-assumptions`.
   - Cache the response in memory for a short time, because the list rarely changes. Cache only successful, non-empty responses, so a CFE outage is never remembered.
4. `other_income`: the 16 fields, conditional value and frequency per type, `total` removed for controlled.
5. Partner equivalents (`partner-blocks.ts`) use the same shapes with the `partner_` prefix: `partner_employment_status`, `partner_incomes`, `partner_receives_benefits`, `partner_benefits`, `partner_*`. Rename `partner_benefits` (yes/no) to `partner_receives_benefits`. Do these in the same pass, reusing the same block factories with a prefix.

### Done when

- `stepsFor` tests: `in_work` adds `income`, `unemployed` does not. `receives_benefits` true adds `benefit_details`. Passported and non-means-tested skip the whole section.
- Saved answers survive `relevantAnswers` (every key is in `STEP_ANSWER_KEYS`).
- `domain/cfe/employment-income` and `irregular-income` build a payload from answers entered through the UI.

### Status: implemented

All five client steps and their partner equivalents now use CCQ's field codes and values. What was built, and where it differs from the plan above:

- **Add-another lists** are generic: `src/eligibility/lists/` holds the row conversion (`add-another.ts`) and the lists (`incomes`, `partner_incomes`, `benefits`, `partner_benefits`, and `bank_accounts`, moved onto the same mechanism). `journey/add-another-step.ts` builds a step from a list. Rows are Add another / Remove / Continue, without JavaScript. A hidden first submit button makes Enter continue rather than click Remove. A row must exist (be added) before its fields are read, so Forge ignores fields posted for rows that were never saved.
- **Frequency lists** are in `domain/frequency-options.ts` and published as `Data("incomeFrequencyOptions")`, `Data("otherIncomeFrequencyOptions")` and `Data("benefitFrequencyOptions")`. Radio `items: Data(...)` works, so the open question is closed. Labels are in that module rather than `locales/en.json`, which the blocks do not read.
- **Partner steps** are built by the same block factories, with an `IncomeSubject` (`journey/income-subject.ts`) for the wording and key prefix. `partner_benefits` (yes/no) is now `partner_receives_benefits`.
- **Benefit types** come from CFE, filtered by `domain/benefit-types.ts`, and are cached in memory for 10 minutes (`registries/benefit-types.ts`). The `StateBenefitType` type now has `name`, `label`, `exclude_from_gross_income`, `dwp_code` and `category`, as CFE returns them. The type is picked from a select, not CCQ's autocomplete.
- **Benefit types unavailable** is a step outside the domain list (`benefit_types_unavailable`). Redirecting from an access hook works (`access({ next: [redirect(...)] })`), but Forge treats a step with no route to it as unreachable, so it needs `reachability: { entryWhen: true }`. The page shows the step, case ID and CFE error, logs the same with `console.error`, and visiting it again retries the call and returns to the benefit step once CFE answers. The `from` query value is only looked up in a fixed list, never used as a path.
- **Dependent fields** (`dependentWhen`) on the other income amount and frequency fields skip validation, and clear the saved value, when the yes/no is not yes.
- **Net income** is checked by the `NetIncomeIsPositive` condition (`domain/income.ts`). It uses exact amounts, where CCQ truncates to whole pounds.
- **Check your answers** shows one line per answered field of each add-another row, and uses `metadata.summaryLabel` where a field's own label is not enough (the other income amounts and frequencies).
- **Amounts** saved from `*_conditional_value` fields and add-another rows are numbers, as the CFE payload builders require.

Tests: `journey/income-section.spec.ts` (routing, validation, what is saved, CFE payloads, the error page, partner), `journey/field-codes.spec.ts` (field codes against `STEP_ANSWER_KEYS`), `lists/add-another.spec.ts`, the domain and registry specs, extra `check-your-answers` cases, and `tests/playwright/tests/eligibility-journey-income.spec.ts`.

Not done, found on the way:

- The older `eligibility-journey-*` Playwright specs (certificated, controlled, domestic abuse, asylum and immigration, under 18, change answers, regression, property, employment and assets) were removed on 2026-10-20. They looked for labels that no longer exist, so none of them could pass. Most only repeated routing that `journey.spec.ts` and `domain/navigation.spec.ts` already test. See [Test strategy](#test-strategy).
- CCQ also checks that a posted frequency or income type is one of the allowed values. Forge's radios do not, so a tampered value is saved as posted.

## Section 2: Property

Independent of income, so it can be done second or in parallel.

### CCQ behaviour

Property steps are spread across two sections. They are interleaved in the final order.

`Steps::OutgoingsSection` contributes (position: after `outgoings` / `partner_outgoings`):

```
property
[property_landlord]       only if property_owned == "shared_ownership"
housing costs step        see Outgoings
```

`Steps::PropertySection` contributes (after housing costs):

```
[property_entry]                        only if the client owns the property
additional_property
[additional_property_details]           only if additional_property_owned is an owned value
[partner_additional_property]           only if partner
[partner_additional_property_details]   only if partner owns an additional property
```

Skip rules:

- Non-means-tested: neither section produces property steps.
- Passported (income skipped but capital not): only `property`, `[property_landlord]`, `property_entry`, and the additional-property steps. No housing costs.

Exits: `property_landlord == false` goes to cannot-use-service. `additional_property_owned == "shared_ownership"` and `partner_additional_property_owned == "shared_ownership"` do the same (`cannotUseService`, already implemented).

| Step | CCQ form | Session keys | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `property` | `PropertyForm` | `property_owned` | Four options, in this order (decision 3): "Yes, with a mortgage or loan" (`with_mortgage`), "Yes, through a shared ownership scheme" (`shared_ownership`), "Yes, owned outright" (`outright`), "No" (`none`). Legend: "Does your client own the home they usually live in?". With a partner: "Does your client or their partner own the home the client usually lives in?". Error: "Select if your client owns the home they live in" |
| `property_landlord` | `PropertyLandlordForm` | `property_landlord` | Boolean. Back link is always `property` (special case in `QuestionFlowController#show`) |
| `property_entry` | `PropertyEntryForm` | `house_value`, `mortgage`, `percentage_owned`, `house_in_dispute` | `house_value > 0`. `mortgage > 0`, required for `with_mortgage` **and** `shared_ownership` (CCQ also hides it otherwise. We show it always, decision 1). `percentage_owned` integer 1 to 100. `house_in_dispute` only when SMOD applies (not immigration or asylum). Heading and hints differ for shared ownership |
| `additional_property` | `AdditionalPropertyForm < PropertyForm` | `additional_property_owned` | Same options as `property`, key prefixed `additional_` |
| `additional_property_details` | `AdditionalPropertyDetailsForm` + `AdditionalPropertyModel` (add-another) | `additional_properties[]` | First row's ownership status comes from `additional_property_owned`. SMOD applies per row |
| `partner_additional_property` | `PartnerAdditionalPropertyForm` | `partner_additional_property_owned` | Prefix `partner_additional_` |
| `partner_additional_property_details` | `PartnerAdditionalPropertyDetailsForm` | `partner_additional_properties[]` | SMOD never applies |

